  {
   "id": 536318,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "A geosynchronous Earth satellite is one that has an orbital period of precisely 1 day. Such orbits are useful for communication and weather observation because the satellite remains above the same point on Earth (provided it orbits in the equatorial plane in the same direction as Earth’s rotation). The ratio <span data-math=\"\\frac{r^3}{T^2}\">\\frac{r^3}{T^2}</span> for the moon is <span data-math=\"1.01\\times10^{18}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}^3}{y^2}\">1.01\\times10^{18}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}^3}{y^2}</span>. Calculate the radius of the orbit of such a satellite.",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1051499,
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"2.75\\times10^3\\,\\text{km}\">2.75\\times10^3\\,\\text{km}</span>"
    },
